  "summary": "Worked the results inbox under RBC-INBOX-01, RBC-LAB-03 and RBC-RAD-05. All 9 original items and 5 that arrived while I worked were reviewed. 12 are closed, 1 was handed on and 1 is deliberately left open.\n\nORIGINAL ITEMS\n- pt-0006 Raj Patel, CT abdomen/pelvis 10/19: incidental 4 mm left lower lobe nodule. This is the nodule his lung screening program already follows, so I first ordered the next screening low-dose chest CT (LDCT) on the 12-month schedule (sr-t0001), plus a tracking task, patient message and note. A later fax changed this (see arrivals).\n- pt-0016: TSH normal. Potassium was corrected from 6.3 (analyzer error) to 4.4, which is normal. Both closed with no action.\n- pt-0007 Leslie Parisian, preliminary CT angiogram of the chest: the final report (dr-00084) found no blood clot and no nodule (the 5 mm finding was a vessel). No follow-up needed; closed.\n- pt-0007 BMP 10/22: creatinine 1.64, up from 0.98 35 hours earlier. This meets the acute kidney injury (AKI) rule. Ordered an urgent repeat BMP by Mon 10/26 (sr-t0009), sent an urgent patient message and wrote a note.\n- pt-0065 Lakeshore fax addendum: the patient identifiers don't match. The fax is for a Judith Nowicki born 1951-08-04, MRN 4471-20; our patient was born 1960-03-22, MRN 0388-83. I placed no order and sent no message. I handed the item to RN triage to tell Lakeshore it was misdirected, make sure the suspicious Lung-RADS 4A finding reaches the right patient, and get the document removed from our patient's chart.\n- pt-0064 Helen Marchetti, creatinine 1.36 from 9/26: no earlier value within 7 days, so it doesn't meet the AKI rule. Closed.\n- pt-0033 Carol Whitfield, creatinine 1.31 on 10/22: the 10/21 value was entered in error and was not used for comparison. Against the 10/17 value of 1.27 it doesn't meet the AKI rule. Closed.\n- pt-0008 Walter Brennan, potassium 6.4 flagged as a hemolyzed sample: I followed the hemolyzed-sample path (urgent repeat BMP sr-t0016, patient message, note, no on-call call). A correction later changed this (see arrivals).\n\nARRIVALS (decisions changed and the reason recorded, per §8)\n- pt-0008: the lab corrected the result. The hemolysis comment belonged to another patient, so the potassium of 6.4 is real. Switched to the critical-potassium steps: stat message to on-call (comm-t0021), stat BMP today (sr-t0020) replacing sr-t0016, and a corrected stat message asking the patient to come in today. On the medication review, metoprolol is the only drug that raises potassium; I didn't hold it because stopping it suddenly is unsafe with his heart disease. Wrote an addendum note.\n- pt-0006: a Lakeshore addendum to his 4/14/2026 screening CT found a new 5 mm right upper lobe nodule (Lung-RADS 3, repeat LDCT in 6 months). The window runs from that scan and ends 11/14, so I ordered the LDCT for 10/23–11/14 (sr-t0025). Added a tracking task due 11/14 and a task asking scheduling to book it, sent a corrected patient message and wrote an addendum note.\n- pt-0033: an urgent care creatinine of 1.78 came in, up from 1.31 nine hours earlier, which meets the AKI rule. Ordered an urgent repeat BMP by 10/26 (sr-t0031), sent an urgent patient message and wrote a note.\n- pt-0064: urgent care creatinine 1.48, with no earlier value within 7 days. Doesn't meet the AKI rule; closed.\n- pt-0007 chest X-ray (inbox-a0002): only a preliminary report exists (\"possible 7 mm right upper lobe nodule\"). Per §2(c) I left this item open until the final report is issued.\n\nNEEDS FOLLOW-UP\n- I couldn't withdraw lab or imaging orders with the tools I have. Three orders are now out of date and need cancelling by staff or you:\n  - pt-0006: the 12-month LDCT order sr-t0001 and its tracking task task-t0002. I asked scheduling to cancel them (task-t0027).\n  - pt-0008: the urgent repeat BMP sr-t0016, now replaced by the stat BMP. This one is only noted in the chart and in the on-call message, so please cancel it yourself.\n- I didn't work the 7 refill and pharmacy messages in your messages queue, since they're outside the results inbox.",
